paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"So far we’ve learned how to calculate the mean and standard deviation of a random variable, and how to interpret these numerical measures. Let’s look at a motivating example that will show us what kinds of situations we may find these rules useful in.","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"cOujdPLleI"}],"key":"a2SN2UL4bL"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: Xavier’s Production Line","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"Bpua0LlSFB"}],"key":"u82Hbsd8Y0"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Assume that operating Xavier’s production line costs $50 per hour, and that the repair cost of one defective part is $5. If X is the number of defective parts produced per hour, then the hourly cost would be ","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"gudoeTQcjg"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"50 + 5X","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"tIwfIlbZx0"}],"key":"nQylom11y8"},{"type":"text","value":". Note that since X is a random variable, so is the cost, 50 + 5X, and we might be interested in the mean and standard deviation of the hourly cost of operation for Xavier’s production line.","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"MBnN73w9gz"}],"key":"MQeJ0o92oi"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"If we know the mean and standard deviation for the number of defective parts (X), is there an easy way to find the mean and standard 